# The judgement in the hall (story "The Nightglass Charge"): Kael kneels bound before Mayor Edrin
# Vale; the accuser and the bought guard press the charge, the defence takes the evidence apart,
# the witnesses are heard, and the mayor judges. Each beat is one side's move; whoever the line
# is put to answers it (the addressee judge), the mayor when it is put to the hall. The verdict
# is played, not scripted: the last beat asks the mayor for his judgement and he gives the one
# the hearing has earned. Needs `arrest-hall-real.png` behind the scene (scanned, named, every
# character with a persona) after `arrest.beats` and `arrest-march.beats` (no reset, but the
# mayor's own).
# speaker | what the speaker sets out to do (their goal; they word it themselves)
@keeper town_mayor
@keeper_situation Presiding from your raised chair in the town hall, the hall full of townsfolk, over the judgement of a sword for hire who kneels bound on the floor before you, charged with stealing the Nightglass dagger from the bladesmith's stall and cutting the watchman who tried to stop him.
@speaker_situation In the town hall before the mayor on his raised chair, the hall full of townsfolk, at the judgement of a sword for hire who kneels bound on the floor, charged with stealing the Nightglass dagger from the bladesmith's stall and cutting the watchman who tried to stop him.
@frames frames=arrest-judgement-a
@frames hold=30
@frames max_frames=10
@frames parallel=2
@frames scene=A plain timber-and-plaster town hall with whitewashed walls, heavy beams, small windows and soft overcast daylight, townsfolk on benches; on a raised wooden chair the stern grey-haired mayor in a plain dark robe and a simple chain leans forward; in the centre the blond bearded man in a fur mantle kneels bound on the floor, the heavy-jawed guard with a moustache pressing his shoulder; the lean dark-haired woman sergeant in a steel cap holds the sealed warrant; at the left the heavy merchant in a dark wool coat with a fur collar holds up a dagger; the four companions and the grey-bearded bladesmith in a leather apron stand at a wooden rail; a sturdy fruit seller woman in a headscarf sits on a bench.
! persona state town_mayor reset
! persona state town_mayor knows warrior
! persona state town_mayor knows market_factor
! persona state town_mayor knows market_guard_lawful
! persona state town_mayor knows market_guard_bought
! persona state town_mayor knows weapon_seller
town_mayor | Have the charge stated before the hall, and ask the kneeling man, as a question to him, how he answers it.
market_factor | Hold the near-black dagger high for the hall, name it the stolen Nightglass taken from the prisoner, and ask the mayor, as a question to him, what more proof any honest town could need.
warrior | From your knees, demand that the bladesmith who forged the Nightglass be let to look at that dagger, and ask the mayor, as a question to him, whether he fears what the smith will say.
town_mayor | Call the bladesmith to the dais and ask him, as a question to him, whether the dagger the accuser holds is the one he forged.
weapon_seller | Turn the dagger in your hands where all can see: its handle sits loose on the tang and its violet marks are painted on the steel, not cut and inlaid as yours are. Tell the mayor plainly what you find, then ask the accuser, as a question to him, where he truly got it.
market_guard_bought | Swear before the whole hall that you saw the prisoner run from the stall with that dagger in his hand, and ask the mayor, as a question to him, whether a guard's oath now counts for nothing.
mara_adventurer | Ask the guard, as a question to him, exactly where he stood when he says he saw Kael run, and what stood between him and the stall.
market_factor | Lay a torn strip of dark cloth at the mayor's feet, saying it was found by the stall, and ask the prisoner, as a question to him, why his own cloak is torn.
warrior | Ask the accuser, as a question to him, who found that strip and when, since no one searched you and nobody saw it taken from you.
market_guard_lawful | Tell the mayor plainly what you saw yourself and what was only reported to you, and ask him, as a question to him, whether the evidence was ever entered with a witness to its finding.
market_witness | Rise from your bench and tell the mayor that the market bell rang late today and you saw the watchman already clutching his bleeding arm before the guards took this man, and ask him, as a question to him, why nobody asked you before.
town_mayor | Press the guard who swore he saw the prisoner run: ask him, as a question to him, at what hour exactly, and how he saw it through the canvas and the crowd.
bram_adventurer | Ask the guard, as a question to him, why his purse is newly heavy this morning, and who filled it.
warrior | Speak for your life: ask the mayor, as a question to him, to judge you by what was shown in this hall today and not by who shouted loudest.
market_factor | Answer the doubt with scorn for the prisoner's friends, and ask the mayor, as a question to him, whether he will let a sword for hire and his hangers-on shame the town's own guard.
town_mayor | Weigh all that was shown and said, and ask the kneeling man, as a question to him, whether he has anything left to say before you judge.
town_mayor | Give your judgement on the charge before the whole hall, as the hearing has earned it, and say what happens now to the accused, the accuser and the guard.
